Argument:
 * Premise : reasoning
 * Conclusion
 * Connection between premise and conclusion is the argument
   - inductive when there is a leap between premises and conclusion
   - dedutive when it is impossible for the conclusion to be false if the premises are all true


* Biases:

 - Belief bias: believing arguments that are more believable from our perspective than based on the reasoning (premises) provided
 - confirmation bias : attaching more weight to evidence that supports our own viewpoint
 - heuristic bias : finding more probable events more believable based on heuristics (using either complete or incomplete data sets)

 - Group Biases:
   - bandwagon effect
   - generalizing from a few anecdotes

 - Negatiivity Bias / Loss aversion: Easier to believe something is wrong than something is right
   So easier to sell the other solutions are bad over the proposed solution is good
   Loss aversion is similar in the sense that it acknowledges people want to minimize loss over maximizing profit


* Persuasion:
  - Winning over somebody to your side or thinking is different from Argument. According to Aristotle, here are different techniques
    of persuasion

    - ethos  : to win over by your credibility / education / list of accomplishments
    - pathos : to connect on a personal basis and win over by emotion / sentiments
    - logos  : sheer logic and argument

   Most commercial advertisements use ethos or pathos.

* Fallacies:

   - Ad Hominem : Attack the person, background, school of thought, organization that conveys the message not the message in itself
   - Straw Man  : Distorting or misrepresenting the original argument into something that can be easily disproved
   
     